speechtext:
I invite the attention of the committee to the resolution on page 12 of the Votes and Proceedings of May 11, concerning bounties on hemp, in regard to which I want to make a change, not as to the amount but as to the distribution of it. I move the resolution, as amended, as follows:
Resolved:
1.	That the Governor in Council may authorize the payment out of the Consolidated Revenue Fund of the following bounties on hem-p grown in Canada and used in the manufacture of yam or twine, or in the manufacture of hemp further advanced than yarn or twine, manufactured in Canada and sold for consumption therein, during the periods and at the rates following, that is to say:
During the calendar year:
1926,	one and one-half cents per pound;
1927,	one and three-eighths cents per pound;
1928,	one and one-fourth cents per pound;
1929,	one and one-eighth cents per pound;
1930,	one cent per pound;
1931,	seven-eighths of one cent per pound;
1932,	three-fourths of one cent per pound;
1933,	one-half of one cent per pound;
2.	That the sum to be paid as such bounty shall not exceed $125,000 in any one of the said periods;
3.	That the said bounty shall not be paid on hemp yarn or twine, or manufactures of hemp further advanced than yarn or twine, exported from Canada;
4.	That the Governor in Council may make regulations to carry out the intention of these resolutione; and
5.	That the Minister of Trade and Commerce shall be charged with the administration of the foregoing provisions.
The resolution originally proposed to provide for a bounty for five years not exceeding in any one year $200,000, making a gross possible appropriation of $1,000,000. I am suggesting that the gross amount remain at that figure, but that we distribute it over eight years, the amount in any one year not to exceed $125,000. That gives a wider distribution, although the bounty is no larger. We propose that the first year shall be 1926.
